### `OpenSslClientSessionCache.java` — freed `NativeSslSession` accessed after `removeSessionWithId()`

When `singleUsed` is true, `removeSessionWithId(nativeSslSession.sessionId())` is called inside the synchronized block (line 124), which chains through `notifyRemovalAndFree` → `NativeSslSession.free()` → `close()`, setting `freed = true` on the native session. The subsequent `nativeSslSession.setLastAccessedTime()` and `nativeSslSession.getCreationTime()` / `getLastAccessedTime()` / `sessionId()` / `keyValueStorage` reads outside the lock were then accessing the freed object. While these particular field reads are currently safe (no native calls, no `assert !freed`), they violate the class's own lifecycle contract — other methods (`session()`, `shouldBeSingleUse()`, `upRef()`) already carry `assert !freed` and the class pattern is clearly meant to prohibit post-free access. Fixed by moving the `setLastAccessedTime` and `setSessionDetails` calls into the `else` branch (only executed when the session was not single-use and thus not freed).

